      "name": "WriteCharToFileBuffer",
      "signature": "undefined WriteCharToFileBuffer(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "return_type": "undefined",
      "comment": "Writes a single character to a FILE stream buffer with automatic flush.\n\nImplements buffered character output for printf-style formatting. Decrements the buffer\ncount and checks for buffer overflow. If the buffer is full, calls __flsbuf to flush the\nbuffer. Otherwise, stores the character in the current buffer position and increments the\npointer. Returns the character on success or EOF (-1) on error. Increments the character\ncounter on successful write.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if the FILE stream has valid flags (0x40 = _IOFBF buffering) AND has a buffer\n2. Decrement the buffer byte count (_cnt field at offset +0x4)\n3. If count < 0 (buffer full): Call __flsbuf to flush and get new buffer space\n4. If count >= 0 (buffer has space): Store character at current _ptr and increment pointer\n5. Check if __flsbuf returned EOF (-1)\n6. If error occurred: Set character counter to -1 and return error code\n7. If successful: Increment the character counter (*pCharCount) and return character value\n\nParameters:\n  charValue - Single byte character to write (passed in AL)\n  pFile - FILE structure pointer containing buffer state (implicit in ECX)\n  pCharCount - Pointer to character count accumulator (implicit in ESI)\n\nReturns:\n  int - The character value written (0-255) on success, or -1 (EOF) on error\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - If FILE stream has no buffer (_base == NULL): Skip write and just increment counter\n  - If __flsbuf returns -1: Stop processing and mark error in counter\n  - Character counter is incremented even on partial writes when buffer is full\n  - Uses FILE structure fields: _flag (flags), _base (buffer), _ptr (write position), _cnt (bytes left)",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
      "method": "NOP",
      "index": "NOP:eb7547bdfd94d81819ab5b9b6872332c245cbb0699bb18a45e61aed4086a6a4f",
